            <ArticleTitle>This chronic patient becomes a humanistic patient who helps clinicians.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>784-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jpainsymman.2012.05.013</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0885-3924(12)00359-4</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A historian of aging, privileged to work with an interdisciplinary team of caregivers and researchers in a division of geriatric and palliative care of a major medical school and teaching hospital, discovers that his history of chronic illnesses secures him a useful role as a humanistic patient who helps clinicians to respond to the concerns, fears, and needs of aging Boomers.</AbstractText>
